WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.133
-Luke, it is great to see you.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.767
Thank you so much for --
Yeah, okay.

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:05.234
Alright. Yeah.

00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:08.434
Ow!

00:00:08.434 --> 00:00:10.234
We have a lot in common.

00:00:10.234 --> 00:00:12.634
[ Laughter ]

00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:14.968
-Do tell.
-Besides looks.

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:16.234
We're identical twins.

00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:17.901
[ Laughter ]

00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:19.634
I know that you're --
You have an Airstream.

00:00:19.634 --> 00:00:21.167
You own an Airstream.
-I do. Yeah.

00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:22.868
-Talk to me about this.

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:26.801
-It was sort of one of those
kind of COVID impulse buys.

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:29.701
My wife and I had just moved to
Austin, Texas.

00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:30.901
You know, we were going to...

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:32.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.200
...put down some roots.
-I love Austin.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.267
-COVID hit, and our really cool
loft became like a jail cell.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:40.234
And we were just
kind of trapped in there

00:00:40.234 --> 00:00:42.000
and figured I always wanted
to do it,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.667
always wanted an Airstream,
always wanted to do that

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.033
big cross-country trip,
so we finally did it.

00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:48.334
-You did it?
-Yeah.

00:00:48.334 --> 00:00:53.434
-When I get my Airstream,
I kind of -- I rent it almost.

00:00:53.434 --> 00:00:55.234
For like a week.

00:00:55.234 --> 00:00:56.501
[ Laughter ]
Or less.

00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:58.467
-I think that means
you don't have an Airstream.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:00.501
-I don't currently have
an Airstream.

00:01:00.501 --> 00:01:01.801
[ Laughter ]

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:03.834
But at one point,
I had one for a week.

00:01:03.834 --> 00:01:08.100
And I had it.
I set it up on a beach.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.501
-Oh, cool, man.
Aren't they the greatest?

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:11.634
It's not the roomiest thing
in the world.

00:01:11.634 --> 00:01:13.434
-I turned into
a different person.

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:16.901
I was like -- I was the dude.
I was the man. I loved it.

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:21.334
I lived in -- My trailer
neighbors were all my buds.

00:01:21.334 --> 00:01:23.067
-Yeah, man.
-We had our -- Yeah.

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:24.934
We had our own thing,
our own deal.

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:26.300
We loved it.
We were sharing food.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.601
-Yeah. That trip sort of became
about, for us, like,

00:01:28.601 --> 00:01:30.767
finding where we actually wanted
to live.

00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:32.234
-Yeah.
-And we went to all

00:01:32.234 --> 00:01:34.434
the mountains all through the
west and ended up in Montana,

00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:37.000
which is where the Airstream
is now parked at our house

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.634
that we built.
-Is that right?

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.434
-Yeah, that was sort of --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:42.234
Kind of homesteaded up there
a little bit.

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:44.267
-You know what we did?
I had this Airstream.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.167
And then to be really honest,
I do own an Airstream

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:50.934
because when the pandemic
happened, we all shut down,

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:53.634
I mentioned, you know,
before on the show

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:55.234
that I love this Airstream,

00:01:55.234 --> 00:02:00.067
and they gave me one to make
into a studio for our show.

00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:01.067
-Oh, cool, man.

00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.200
-To do the show
from my backyard.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.133
-Amazing.
-Yeah. So it was because of

00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:07.467
Airstream that we got to
actually do the show

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:09.667
for a couple episodes, so thank
you, Airstream, for doing that.

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:11.701
-Wow.
-It was a wild, bizarre thing.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:14.200
But I never changed it. I kept
it as a television studio.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.667
-So basically all through COVID,
you and I were in Airstreams.

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.534
We are the same person, bro.

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:20.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:23.501
-Is there any downside
to traveling just living

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:25.934
on the road on an Airstream?

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:28.868
-The only downside was
that we have a cat.

00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:32.934
And it's a Bengal cat, so it's
kind of like a high-tech cat.

00:02:32.934 --> 00:02:34.267
It's not like a normal --

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:35.901
-What does a high-tech cat mean?

00:02:35.901 --> 00:02:38.133
-It's just got more going on
than a house cat

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:40.067
that sits there
and licks itself or whatever.

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:42.701
It's got a lot of energy.

00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:45.767
That was tough. It did not like
the confined space.

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:47.267
-What did you have to do?

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:49.234
-Well, at first, we let it
kind of run around,

00:02:49.234 --> 00:02:51.133
and then coyotes
were coming around,

00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:52.701
and, you know,
that wasn't very good.

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:55.334
So my wife got a leash
for our cat.

00:02:55.334 --> 00:02:59.701
And turns out, it's not
very easy to walk a cat.

00:02:59.701 --> 00:03:01.200
-I was going to say,
can you walk a cat?

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.567
Is that possible?
-You can.

00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:07.868
Just not near trees or anything
that they can climb.

00:03:07.868 --> 00:03:09.501
-They climb trees?
-Yeah.

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:12.501
-Well, obviously, cat in
the tree or whatever --

00:03:12.501 --> 00:03:13.534
That actually happens?

00:03:13.534 --> 00:03:14.901
-Yeah, that old song and dance.

00:03:14.901 --> 00:03:16.300
-Yeah, I've seen it in cartoons,

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.734
but they really do
climb up trees?

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:20.300
-Oh, yeah. She'd be sitting
there just holding the leash.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.667
-You brought a --
You actually have a picture.

00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:24.634
-Yeah, proof.
-Here's your cat. Wow.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:26.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.434 --> 00:03:28.701
That really did happen.

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:31.033
You ended up getting it out of
the tree, I'm assuming.

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:32.534
-Yeah, yeah. We got her back.
We got her back.

00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:36.400
-No. You left her there.
-She's still in that tree.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.868
-Not only do you star in the
biggest show on television,

00:03:38.868 --> 00:03:40.634
but you have your
first country music

00:03:40.634 --> 00:03:42.100
single out right now, buddy.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.367
Look at this.
"No Horse to Ride."

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:47.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.167
Congratulations.
-Thanks, man.

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:51.934
-How did this all come about?

00:03:51.934 --> 00:03:54.601
-I've always played music.
Music sort of came first.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.934
Yeah. I grew up in church
and been playing music

00:03:56.934 --> 00:03:59.567
since I was probably
10 or 11 years old.

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:01.000
Just something I always
stuck with.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.601
I was always in bands
when I lived in L.A.,

00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:05.701
and someone kind of caught wind
that I was writing some stuff,

00:04:05.701 --> 00:04:07.133
and one thing led to another.

00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:08.868
And I got a record deal,
so here we are.

00:04:08.868 --> 00:04:09.868
-Wow.

00:04:09.868 --> 00:04:12.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:14.734
"No Horse to Ride"
is out right now.

00:04:14.734 --> 00:04:16.901
Are you going to do an album?

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:19.067
-Yes.
I start recording February.

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:21.400
Should be about probably
end of March-ish.

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:22.601
-No way!
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:24.601
-Go, Luke Grimes.
That's what I'm talking about.

00:04:24.601 --> 00:04:26.367
[ Cheers and applause ]
Dude, congratulations, man.

00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:27.634
-Thanks, dude.
-It's all happening.

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:28.634
-Thanks, man.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:29.968
-Can we talk about
"Yellowstone"?

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:32.501
Because it's the biggest thing
to happen

00:04:32.501 --> 00:04:34.601
in television since "Seinfeld."

00:04:34.601 --> 00:04:36.100
I don't know what else to say.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.868
It's a giant, giant show
that everyone is watching.

00:04:38.868 --> 00:04:41.367
There's a lot of drama going on
right now

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:43.200
between your character's
siblings,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.367
and I don't want to spoil
anything.

00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:48.400
But looks like you're not going
to spoil anything either.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.868
-Lips are sealed.

00:04:49.868 --> 00:04:51.467
-Yeah, you're really good
at this stuff.

00:04:51.467 --> 00:04:53.834
Do you know what's
going to happen?

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:55.334
-No, I don't.

00:04:55.334 --> 00:04:58.434
I think some of the cast
know the end.

00:04:58.434 --> 00:05:00.234
Some have been told.
Some haven't.

00:05:00.234 --> 00:05:02.033
-You don't want to know.
-I don't. I don't.

00:05:02.033 --> 00:05:04.968
And I don't think Taylor,
who writes our show,

00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:06.400
wants me to know either.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.000
I don't know.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.434
It might affect the way you do
something or play something.

00:05:10.434 --> 00:05:13.267
And it's kind of fun to
experience it this way anyway.

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:15.734
It's sort of like life.

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:17.400
-How do you describe
your character?

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.167
I've heard descriptions saying
your character is kind of

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:23.601
like Michael Corleone
in "The Godfather."

00:05:23.601 --> 00:05:26.634
-Yeah, that's been kind of
a reference throughout,

00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:28.100
but we'll have to see.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.000
I think that's what we're all
kind of waiting to see.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.701
Can he kind of step up and man
up and do the things necessary

00:05:33.701 --> 00:05:35.901
to help the family
keep the place or not?

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:38.701
I think that's what we're all
kind of watching to find out.

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:40.834
-I want to show everyone a clip.
Here's Luke Grimes

00:05:40.834 --> 00:05:42.934
in the midseason finale of
"Yellowstone."

00:05:42.934 --> 00:05:44.501
Take a look at this.

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:47.734
-Can you come tomorrow
and get the rest on the road?

00:05:47.734 --> 00:05:49.334
-Let me just ask my wife.

00:05:50.901 --> 00:05:52.934
-Yeah, you do that, son.

00:05:52.934 --> 00:05:54.934
Whatever she says, stick to it.

00:05:54.934 --> 00:05:58.667
-Yeah, tends to be
the safest move.

00:06:00.701 --> 00:06:02.467
-You're stronger than I was.

00:06:05.467 --> 00:06:07.200
Guess that's how it's supposed
to be.

00:06:11.067 --> 00:06:12.801
I love you, son.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:17.501
-Come on, now.

00:06:17.501 --> 00:06:19.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.834
Who's cooler than this guy?

00:06:22.567 --> 00:06:28.901
Luke, before you go, now you're
a country music star.

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:30.234
You're a star of "Yellowstone"
now.

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:32.767
I thought we'd put you to a test
in a new game called

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:35.000
"Line or Lyric:
Yellowstone Edition."

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.567
Okay? Here's how this works.

00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:37.968
I'm going to read a line off
this card.

00:06:37.968 --> 00:06:40.334
You have to tell me if it's
dialogue from "Yellowstone"

00:06:40.334 --> 00:06:42.400
or if it's
a country music lyric.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:43.701
-[ Laughs ] Okay.

00:06:43.701 --> 00:06:45.367
-Then we'll show you video of
the correct answer.

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:46.534
You ready for this?
-Yeah. Yeah, let's do it.

00:06:46.534 --> 00:06:47.968
-Alright. Here's the first one.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.033
"You are the trailer park,
I am the tornado."

00:06:54.033 --> 00:06:56.467
-That is Beth Dutton for sure.
100%.

00:06:56.467 --> 00:06:58.467
-You think Beth Dutton 100%?
-100%.

00:06:58.467 --> 00:06:59.567
-It is not
a country music lyric?

00:06:59.567 --> 00:07:01.000
-That is my final answer.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.467
-"You are the trailer park."
♪ I am the torn-- ♪

00:07:03.467 --> 00:07:05.801
Alright, well, let's see
if he's right.

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:10.534
-You are the trailer park.
I am the tornado.

00:07:10.534 --> 00:07:13.267
[ Ding! ]

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:14.868
-Beth Dutton!

00:07:14.868 --> 00:07:16.267
How awesome is Beth Dutton?

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:17.868
Alright, here we go.
How about this one?

00:07:17.868 --> 00:07:20.300
"I should've been a cowboy.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.467
I should've learned
to rope and ride."

00:07:22.467 --> 00:07:24.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:24.601 --> 00:07:26.367
-That's a country song.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:28.968
-Let me see.

00:07:28.968 --> 00:07:32.601
-♪ I should've been a cowboy ♪

00:07:32.601 --> 00:07:34.534
♪ Should've learned
to rope and ride ♪

00:07:34.534 --> 00:07:36.667
-Back in the mullet days.
-Mullet days.

00:07:36.667 --> 00:07:38.767
Yeah, Toby Keith right there.
Alright.

00:07:38.767 --> 00:07:40.267
How about this one?

00:07:41.567 --> 00:07:44.734
"We got so much to talk about.
Why aren't we talkin'?"

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:48.901
-"Yellowstone"?

00:07:50.534 --> 00:07:52.133
-Is it "Yellowstone" or is it
country music lyric?

00:07:52.133 --> 00:07:53.634
-Can I ask the audience?

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:55.033
-We got a lot to talk about.

00:07:55.033 --> 00:07:56.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:00.534 --> 00:08:02.901
-We got so much to talk about,
why aren't we talkin'?

00:08:02.901 --> 00:08:04.400
[ Ding! ]
-That's exactly right!

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.400
"Why aren't we talkin'?"
That's you.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.267
Casey Dutton, Luke Grimes,
everybody.

00:08:09.267 --> 00:08:12.400
"Yellowstone" returns this
summer on Paramount Network.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.067
We'll be right back with
a performance from Protoje.

00:08:15.067 --> 00:08:16.300
Stick around, everybody!

